Signatures with 16 KiB hash pages on the 4 KiB kernel, authenticated at first use: boot argument cs-lazy=1.

Without the argument nothing changes: an image is read to its code limit when its signature is added and the
table of 4 KiB hashes is made then. With it, adding a signature reads nothing. An original 16 KiB block is
authenticated when one of its 4 KiB pages is first validated: its pages are paged in one after the other and
copied into a private buffer, the buffer is hashed and compared with the block's slot of the code directory, and
only on a match the hashes of its 4 KiB parts, made from that same buffer, are recorded. A page is validated
against its recorded hash as before, so nothing is accepted that the signed 16 KiB hash does not cover. The
argument is read once at boot, so a blob is of one form for its life; a blob whose hash is longer than 32 bytes
keeps the eager form.

A block has one of three answers. Ready: its derived hashes are recorded, written once and final. Bad: its
buffer did not match the signed hash; its pages are answered tainted, unless another thread's buffer matched
and made it ready. No answer yet: a validation answers "not validated, not tainted", as for a page without a
hash, which accepts nothing and marks no page, so the page is validated again on its next use.

The VM asks before it validates (vm_fault_cs_lazy_pending, object locked): vm_fault_page, when it has found
the page and before it takes it, drops the page, the locks and the top page, keeps the object and its pager
with a reference and a paging reference, authenticates and returns VM_FAULT_RETRY, which covers the slow path,
the copies inside vm_fault_page, vm_object_copy_slowly and the source pages and peeks of every pager; the fast
path of vm_fault leaves such a page to the slow path; vm_fault_wire_fast returns KERN_FAILURE so that vm_fault
takes it; the Mach-O loader authenticates the header blocks before it checks them. vm_fault_enter, should it
still meet such a block, enters nothing and returns KERN_ABORTED. The low-memory substitution of the I/O page
list copies an unvalidated page into the same signed object, where the copy is validated when it is mapped.
A block that cannot be read fails the fault with KERN_MEMORY_ERROR and leaves no answer.

The pages of a block are fetched through vm_fault_page from the vnode's VM object (vm_fault_cs_lazy_fetch), one
at a time, with no VM lock and no busy page held by the authentication, never through VNOP_READ.

The answers live in chunks of 127 blocks (16 KiB per 1.98 MiB of file) that are allocated at the first
authentication in them and found through a table of chunk pointers. Table and chunks are mapped read-only
before they can be reached and are written through the physical aperture, as the read-only zone allocator
writes its elements on a kernel without a monitor; the table pointer is a field of the blob in that zone.

Instead of the refusal of a whole image at load: the structural checks are unchanged, an image shorter than its
code limit is refused, and a block that differs from its signed hash is found when it is first used.

sysctl vm.cs_native4k_stats gains: lazy <0|1> blobs, auth (blocks authenticated), bad, err, ms, "retry" per
site (page, fast, wire, load, enter) and "unready" (validations that met a block without an answer: total, map,
pager, reval, range), which must stay 0.

Has not run on hardware.
